# Phase 01: Compound Learning Retrieval Bridge

## Overview
Wire `brainstorm_claims` + `brainstorm_mechanisms` tables into `CompoundCache.lookup()` so ThinkRunner automatically retrieves past analysis claims — not just decision_vectors.

## Problem
- `post_analysis_store.py` WRITES to `brainstorm_claims` table
- `CompoundCache.lookup()` READS from `decision_vectors` table only
- Two tables never connected → each analysis starts from zero

## Files to Modify
- `D:/code/invesments/master_investments/.claude/lib/intelligence/decomposition/compound_cache.py`

## Implementation Steps

1. Add method `_query_brainstorm_claims(dimension, context)` to CompoundCache
   - Query `brainstorm_claims` table WHERE dimension matches (with alias mapping)
   - Filter by 180-day freshness (same as decision_vectors)
   - Return as ParsedClaim objects compatible with existing return type

2. Add method `_query_brainstorm_mechanisms(context)` to CompoundCache
   - Query `brainstorm_mechanisms` for matching topic/industry
   - Return mechanism + bottleneck + money_flow as context enrichment

3. Update `lookup()` to merge both sources
   - Call existing `_query_decision_vectors()` 
   - Call new `_query_brainstorm_claims()`
   - Deduplicate by fact text similarity (>90% match = duplicate)
   - Return merged list, sorted by confidence DESC

4. Handle case where brainstorm tables don't exist yet (graceful fallback)

5. Write tests in `tests/test_compound_cache_bridge.py`
   - Test: brainstorm_claims retrieved alongside decision_vectors
   - Test: deduplication works
   - Test: graceful fallback when brainstorm tables missing
   - Test: 180-day freshness filtering applies to brainstorm_claims

## Success Criteria
- [ ] `CompoundCache.lookup()` returns claims from BOTH tables
- [ ] Existing tests still pass
- [ ] New test file passes
- [ ] Second ThinkRunner run on same topic shows cached claims from first run
